00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
there are many myths about consulting  one example of things i  frequently hear is that often in  consulting projects  the result is already decided at the  beginning of the project  meaning the consultants just write down  the things that the management of the  client is warning them to write down  anyway  is all of this true these are the things  that i'm going to discuss  in this video today i'm reviewing the  three most common myths  that i frequently heard about consulting  so welcome to another video here on my  channel  firm learning today it's quite warm here  in munich so i'm not drinking a coffee  but just the water  hope that's fine for you as well as you  know my name is heinrich and on my  channel i want to help you  to become successful in the first years  of your career but now without further  ado let's  jump into the three myths and let's have  a look whether these are actually true  or not  so let's start with myth number one  about consulting and this is likely the  thing that i hear most  frequently when people talk badly about  consultants  and this is that consultants just write  down what other people on the client  side  tell them to write down anyway so pretty  much implying that the results of the  project  are already clear are already defined  from the beginning of the project  and consultants are just there to make  it look good  just to provide justification for maybe  some harsh  measures that the management wants to  take anyway and what these people mean  is that sometimes  there is senior management maybe of a  client this senior management wants to  do some harsh measures with the  organization  often this will be related to cost  cutting maybe even deciding to let go of  employees of people in the organization  and they don't want to do that on  themselves  they just ask consultants to come in and  just to do a project to investigate  these things  and then in the end just to write down  what they wanted to do in the first  place so what is  my view on that and in general  personally i consider this perspective  to be flawed  i never experienced a situation where at  the beginning of a project it was  clear what the result was supposed to be  i never was in a meeting with a partner  or any other senior personnel of a  consulting firm  telling the team explaining to the team  that this is now the task  this is what we need to write on piece  of paper this is what the client wants  of course that being said sometimes you  find yourself in a situation  where the client already has a couple of  hypothesis that the client shares with  you so for instance yes it might be that  the client is looking into cost cutting  measures  and is asking you as a consultant to  evaluate these cost-cutting measures  and then of course depending on the  agreed scope the result of that might be  that the new as a consultant also  proposed things  but in my perspective this is something  very different  than just writing down what the client  tells you in the beginning  of course you're going to pick up some  ideas that the clients has  of course you will evaluate them  potentially also incorporate them in  your final suggestion  though what is important to understand  is that if a consulting firm delivers a  recommendation to the client and this is  of course especially true for mbb  consulting firms  like mckinsey bcg or bain the reputation  of your firm will be very much attached  to the quality of these recommendations  just as an example i  in the past worked for a client for a  firm  and several years ago they apparently  did a project with boston consulting  group with bcg  and bcg developed a strategy for this  client  and this document was still being  circulated through the organization so  this bcg strategy document  and everyone just called the document  yeah the bcg document the bcg strategy  so clearly your name your reputation  is often very directly tied to the  results of your work  and clearly big consulting firms just  would not be able to justify to  themselves  just to potentially damage their  reputation do  things write things down that they do  not believe are true that they do not  believe our recommendation only because  any client is asking them  to do that this is just not how it works  let's talk about the second consulting  myth and this is that consulting is  nothing else than glorified temp  work this would mean that as a  consultant you are not  really developing new concepts and  strategies as an independent party  but you are in the end nothing else and  just an extension of the workforce for  the employer for your client so maybe if  a client needs some  extra capacity some extra things that  need to get done  but the client does not have enough  capacity to get this done in-house  this will be then the scenario where the  client is asking for consultants  just to take them on as temporary  workers to do the things that they  cannot currently do in their home  organization  and my perspective on this is that while  there might be some truth to that  let me come to that in a moment what  that would be i would argue that this is  largely completely exaggerated and not  really the case as a consultant the norm  is specially talking about  mbb consulting firms for mckinsey bcg  and bain  you pride yourself of delivering an  independent opinion  of delivering your analysis your view on  the perspective that you bring to the  client indeed in a normal consulting  setup you are not  directly reporting to the client in the  sense that the client will tell you what  you have to do  of course the client will want to review  your work will discuss  this work with you but usually this will  not take the form of the client exactly  telling you what the individual steps  are  that they want you to complete and for  sure again this is custom myth number  one  they will not tell you what the results  of your work are supposed to be so in  general i disagree with this myth and  this was even something that was also  top of mind of many partners i used to  work with  that they always used to say so what we  don't want to do  is just to be the extended workbench of  the client so just to  get some work done get tasks done that  at the moment the client cannot complete  it should always be a part of your  independent opinion  your perspective that you bring to the  table so that being said i think there  are two perspectives on this  where maybe you could come to the  conclusion that yes there might be some  truth to that  and the first is that the things that i  talked about are very much true for mbb  consulting  but might be less true for other  consulting firms maybe especially  smaller or boutique consulting firms so  while i don't want to say that this is  usually the case there might be some  consulting firms  where your work often is indeed rather  compared to temp work  than to actually providing an  independent opinion and the second  perspective of this is that sometimes  especially in projects  that already ongoing for long time  projects that got extension after  extension after extension  often you are really coming with a  client into really a working mode and  operational mode  where you do tasks agree on next steps  agree on to do's  so these are the situations where then  sometimes the lines get a little bit  blurry  and here also the client just might like  to work with you in this setting  because even though your fees probably  are not really economical  to just use you as a normal employee  equivalent  for them maybe they just don't have the  budget approval not the means not to  hire new people  and it might just be easier for the  client to continue working with you as a  consultant  even though your fees are much higher  than what they would pay to an employee  just out of the convenience aspect of it  so let's look at  myth number three and this is that  consultants actually don't care about  their clients  they just want to make as much money as  possible they just want to collect their  consulting  fees their per diems from the clients  and whether what they do then is  creating any actual value at all they  don't even care about that as long as  they get  paid and i find this way of talking  about this quite  bitter to be honest so surely consulting  firms as  every other firm is a business after all  and will need to think economically  but that this just means that  consultants don't care about their  clients and customers  this just seems very wrong to me and  just to illustrate that so  at my former employer one of the highest  values kind of the highest evolution  level of being a consultant  is really to become a trusted advisor to  the clients to the  senior management to the people that you  work with so whenever one of these  people would have  a problem a business related issue they  wanted to discuss  ideally they would think about you top  of their mind  about a person that they can sit down  with just use you as a sparing partner  discuss some topics with you  and then potentially also then start a  project to address certain issues  so surely deeply caring about your  clients  deeply being concerned about what really  creates  value at your clients and has a positive  impact this  from my experience from what i have seen  was always at the core  of every successful partner i worked  with i'm  not even once experienced a situation  where  a partner or any other senior person of  the consulting firm  would just barely imply anything like oh  this project probably will not go away  but i don't care because i will make  lots of money so that's fine  like not even once in six years working  consulting ever heard  anything like this and again it's of  course difficult now to speak  for every single consulting firm out  there of course  my experiences are very much influenced  by the firm that i worked for  but you know i would assume that at  least for the mbb firms mckinsey bcg  bain  this will be the trend this is the  situation  that you will face and this was also  very well reflected in the culture and  also the whole incentive system so you  were also evaluated even as a junior  consultant  so you would always be rewarded if you  were able to come up with good ideas  that really  helped the client if you came up with  something that really  resonated had a positive impact that  really satisfied the client  made an impact these were the things  that would make you be perceived  as a strong consultant as a rock star  and frankly if you set up your work in a  way that you don't care about your  client success  but just about your own success i think  this will be  very very difficult because often it's  just the truth  these two are very closely interlinked  so the one regularly leads to the other  and the other way around  which i think great because this just  means that the incentives are aligned  so what do you think about these big  myths about consulting do you think they  are true  what are your reflections on that would  love to hear your perspective  in the video comments to know whether  you agree with what i've said or not  as always if you took any value out of  this video at all please hit the like  button for the youtube algorithm  and also subscribe to my channel to stay  up to date on all my content  i'm also active on other channels such  as instagram ticktalk or twitch  you find the links to these channels in  the video description of course as  always also big thanks to all the  members of firm learning  you're really making a great  contribution to the course thank you for  that  my name is heine from from learning was  a pleasure doing this video for you  see you again next week good weekend to  all of you and bye bye

